<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dzibanche</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dzibanché</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dzibanche is a Maya archaeological site in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintana-roo">Quintana Roo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, occupied from the late Preclassic period. Inscriptions show that it was the seat of the Kaan, or snake-head, dynasty in the 5th and 6th centuries CE, before the dynasty moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a> in the 7th century. Its buildings include the Temple of the Owl and the Temple of the Captives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dzibanche.jpg" alt="Dzibanche" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dlegloire · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dzibanche_estructura_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The city lies 130 kilometres north-east of Chetumal, a short distance inland from the Bacalar Lagoon, on raised ground surrounded by a bajo, a seasonal swamp with fertile soils. Its ancient name was Kaanu'l, place of snakes, from which the ruling dynasty took its name. The modern name means writing on wood and was given after Thomas Gann rediscovered the site in 1927 and found carved wooden lintels in the Temple of the Lintels. With its farmland the city covers over 60 square kilometres, in four groups, the Main Group, Tutil, Lamay and Kinichná, joined by sacbe causeways that also lead to the city of Ichkabal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Settlement began around 300 BCE. Between the late 4th and early 5th centuries the Kaanu'l dynasty began a military expansion through conquest and alliance, subordinating nearby sites such as El Resbalón, Los Alacranes, Pol Box and Dzibantunich and allying with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naranjo">Naranjo</a> and Caracol. The hieroglyphic stairway holds the earliest known use of the Kaan emblem glyph, dated to 495 CE. The expansion brought conflict with Tikal, which Dzibanche defeated in 562.</p><p>After part of the lineage moved its seat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a>, the branch that stayed went on building and winning victories over nearby sites. Dzibanche was among the last Maya cities to carve a dated hieroglyphic text, in 909, and was abandoned around the 11th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Main Group, the royal core, is laid out around the Gann, Xibalba and Pom plazas. The Temple of the Owl holds a tomb of about 400 CE with the remains of a high-ranking woman, named after a vessel with an owl-shaped lid; the Temple of the Cormorants, the largest pyramid, holds the tomb of the ruler Sky Witness, who reigned from 572 to 598. The Temple of the Captives shows bound prisoners taken by Yuknoom Chʼeen I. The 35-metre K'inich Na' pyramid stands about 2 kilometres north of the core.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dzibanche” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its great rival was Kaanul, the Snake kingdom, based first at <b>Dzibanche</b> and later at Calakmul, which built a network of allies around Tikal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tikal">Tikal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The two greatest powers, Tikal and the Snake kingdom of Kaanul, ruled from <b>Dzibanche</b> and later from Calakmul, contended for supremacy through much of the 6th to 8th centuries, drawing in allies from Caracol to Dos Pilas and Naranjo.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The snake-head emblem dynasty of Kaan is first firmly attested at <b>Dzibanche</b> in southern Quintana Roo in the 5th and 6th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kaan">Kingdom of Kaan</a></li></ul></section>
